<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  FUEL: REBUILT's (FRC 2026) scoring element.

  The three figures that are NOT opinion come from the game manual, section 5.10.1:

    - diameter 5.91 in = 15.0 cm            ->  radius 0.075 m
    - mass     0.448-0.500 lb = 0.203-0.227 kg  ->  nominal 0.215 kg
    - "high density foam ball", AndyMark am-5801

  The color is the official field model's: it is the baseColorFactor of the
  material its 456 Fuel meshes use, read from the .glb, not an orange picked
  by eye.

  The friction is a STARTING POINT, not a measurement; it is explained below
  and calibrated with worlds/fuel-drop.sdf.

  Bounce does NOT exist: this gz-physics build ignores <bounce>. It is
  measured, not assumed --- see the comment inside <surface>.
-->
<sdf version="1.11">
  <model name="fuel">
    <link name="link">

      <!--
        Solid sphere: I = 2/5 * m * r^2 = 0.4 * 0.215 * 0.075^2 = 4.8375e-4.

        A foam ball is practically homogeneous, so the solid sphere is the
        right model and not a simplification. Putting a made-up inertia here
        matters more than it looks: it is what decides how much it spins when
        rubbing against an intake roller.
      -->
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.215</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>4.8375e-4</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>4.8375e-4</iyy><iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>4.8375e-4</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry><sphere><radius>0.075</radius></sphere></geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <!--
                0.85. High-density foam grips more than smooth plastic and
                less than a wheel's rubber; the FRC carpet on the floor is set
                to 0.9. This value is what decides whether the FUEL rolls or
                drags when pushed, and whether an intake drags it in or makes
                it slip.
              -->
              <mu>0.85</mu>
              <mu2>0.85</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
          <!--
            THERE IS NO BOUNCE, and it is not an oversight.

            There used to be a <bounce><restitution_coefficient>0.35</...>
            here. It was removed after MEASURING it: with
            `bridge/src/bin/fuel-bounce.rs` against worlds/fuel-drop.sdf, the
            three balls give e = 0.000 with both DART and bullet-featherstone.
            This gz-physics build ignores <bounce> entirely.

            Leaving it in would have been worse than not having it: a number
            that can be "tuned" for a whole afternoon without anything
            changing.

            Practical consequence: the FUEL drops and stays. For a foam ball on
            carpet that is fairly close to reality, but it is NO use for
            studying a shot that bounces off the HUB's rim. Getting real
            bounce requires setting the restitution per contact from a C++
            system (gz-physics exposes SetContactPropertiesCallbackFeature);
            that would be work for MarsLink, not an SDF tag.
          -->
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry><sphere><radius>0.075</radius></sphere></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.38 0.24 0.02 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.956 0.597 0.056 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
